  {
    "gold": "11000",
    "question": "James decides to replace his car.  He sold his $20,000 car for 80% of its value and then was able to haggle to buy a $30,000 sticker price car for 90% of its value.  How much was he out of pocket?",
    "question_sha256": "b176ac52d47cc5556d6389125d9fb3f560ea516c269a80f39bb092ff95dccfa7",
    "responses": [
      {
        "key": "gsm8k-train-177:rich",
        "raw_call_sha256": "e4334b4eb472432d8e5dcb184a5e9153e444829780f2d7530e86042dcb8b2077",
        "student_prefix": [
          {
            "content": "James decides to replace his car.  He sold his $20,000 car for 80% of its value and then was able to haggle to buy a $30,000 sticker price car for 90% of its value.  How much was he out of pocket?",
            "role": "user"
          }
        ],
        "student_prefix_sha256": "5ff3e89b1e8ee65d10e90c99058f1c9bcbb743c9cfb116c21926ac833a7bac5f",
        "target": "First, I calculate how much James sold his old car for. He sold it for 80% of its $20,000 value. So, 0.80 * $20,000 = $16,000. \n\nNext, I determine the price James paid for the new car. He bought it for 90% of its $30,000 sticker price. So, 0.90 * $30,000 = $27,000.\n\nTo find out how much James was out of pocket, I subtract the amount he got from selling his old car from the price he paid for the new car. So, $27,000 - $16,000 = $11,000.\n\nFINAL: $11,000",
        "target_sha256": "c8ac0a7741deb3d8cdfb64a9f2920f7ec08bb82d5ddd93c4e66d0c475334515b"
      }
