        "target": "Given that Kiaan needs to deliver newspapers to 200 homes, I first calculated the number of homes he had delivered to after the first hour. Since he delivered to \\( \\frac{2}{5} \\) of the homes, I used the operation \\( \\frac{2}{5} \\times 200 = 80 \\) homes. This left \\( 200 - 80 = 120 \\) homes remaining. \n\nNext, I considered the second part of his delivery. After another 2 hours, he delivered to 60 percent of the remaining 120 homes. I used the operation \\( 0.60 \\times 120 = 72 \\) homes. This left \\( 120 - 72 = 48 \\) homes still needing newspapers. \n\nTo verify my result, I checked that the total number of homes delivered to (80 + 72) plus the remaining homes (48) equals the original 200 homes. This check is \\( 80 + 72 + 48 = 200 \\), which confirms my solution is correct.\n\nThe operation I would reuse is the calculation of the remaining homes after a certain percentage has been delivered. This can be applied to any similar problem where a percentage of a remaining quantity is known and needs to be subtracted.\n\nFINAL: 48",
